    stem: "Which sentence contains an error in its construction and should be revised?\n\n(1) Marcus had always wanted to visit the Museum of Natural History, so his parents surprised him with tickets for his birthday. \n(2) Rushing through the entrance, the dinosaur fossils were the first things Marcus noticed. \n(3) He spent nearly two hours in the fossil hall, sketching the bones in his notebook. \n(4) By the end of the day, Marcus had decided that paleontology was his favorite subject in science.",
    solutionExplanation: "Sentence 1 is not the answer. The two independent clauses are properly joined with a comma and the coordinating conjunction \"so.\" There is no error in construction.  Sentence 2 is correct. This sentence contains a dangling modifier. The opening phrase \"Rushing through the entrance\" must describe the subject of the sentence — but the subject is \"the dinosaur fossils,\" and fossils cannot rush anywhere. The rule is simple: whenever a sentence opens with an -ing phrase, the very next noun must be the person doing that action. A corrected version would read: \"Rushing through the entrance, Marcus immediately noticed the dinosaur fossils.\"  Sentence 3 is not the answer. The participial phrase \"sketching the bones in his notebook\" correctly modifies Marcus, who is the subject of the sentence. There is no error in construction.  Sentence 4 is not the answer. The opening phrase \"By the end of the day\" correctly modifies Marcus, who is the subject of the main clause. A long opening phrase is not automatically an error.",
